<%- include('partials/head') %>
  <body>
<%- include('partials/nav') %>

    <main>
      <h2>My Open Source Story</h2>

      <figure>
        <img src="/img/merged-pr.png" alt="My merged pull request on the LFortran GitHub page">
        <figcaption>My first merged pull request, to LFortran</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <article>
        <h3>CodePath AI301</h3>
        <p>
          This past summer I took CodePath's AI Open Source Capstone. The whole course
          is one loop: find a real issue in a real open source project, fix it, get it
          merged, then pick another one. We met every week in small groups for
          standups, so there was always someone to ask when I got stuck.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article>
        <h3>Finding an issue</h3>
        <p>
          This was harder than I expected. I looked at around 60 issues before I found
          one I could claim. Most of them already had someone working on a fix, even
          when the issue looked open. I learned to check for open pull requests first,
          before reading anything else.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article>
        <h3>My first merged fix</h3>
        <p>
          My first contribution was to LFortran, a compiler for the Fortran language.
          It was accepting a line of code that should have been an error and quietly
          ignoring part of it. I don't know Fortran, but the bug was really about
          error handling, so I could still work on it. I added a check so the compiler
          shows an error instead.
        </p>
        <p>
          By the time my fix was ready, the main project had changed a lot, and I had
          to update my copy and sort out conflicts before it could go in. The
          maintainers merged it, and now my code is part of the project.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article>
        <h3>What I learned</h3>
        <p>
          Open source is as much about people as it is about code. You're working with
          maintainers you've never met, so you read how the project does things,
          introduce yourself when you claim an issue, and keep your pull request small
          and easy to review.
        </p>
      </article>

      <section class="sources">
        <h3>Sources</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="https://www.codepath.org/">CodePath</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://github.com/lfortran/lfortran/pull/12058">My merged pull request to LFortran</a></li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </main>

